                </div>
                <h1 class="content-title">Claiming Your Free PDI &amp; <span class="gradient-text">Navigating the UI</span></h1>
                <p class="content-subtitle">
                    Get your own 100% free Personal Developer Instance (PDI) from ServiceNow, master the 10-day hibernation rules, 
                    and take a comprehensive guided tour of the modern Next Experience user interface.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 1: What is a PDI? -->
            <section class="content-section" id="what-is-pdi">
                <h2>What is a Personal Developer Instance (PDI)?</h2>
                <p>
                    Unlike many enterprise software ecosystems that require expensive corporate licenses just to learn, 
                    ServiceNow provides a <strong>100% free, fully functional, cloud-hosted enterprise sandbox</strong> called a 
                    <strong>Personal Developer Instance (PDI)</strong> to anyone who registers on their developer portal.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Your PDI comes pre-loaded with sample demo data (users, servers, laptops, incidents), full <code>admin</code> superuser rights, 
                    and all major application modules enabled.
                </p>

                <div class="info-card info-card--tip">
                    <div class="info-card-title">You Have Full Admin Superpowers</div>
                    <p>
                        In your PDI, you have complete administrative authority: you can create custom tables, write scripts, build flows, 
                        break things, and reset the instance back to factory settings whenever you want.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 2: Step-by-Step Claim Guide -->
            <section class="content-section" id="step-by-step-claim">
                <h2>Step-by-Step: How to Claim Your Free PDI</h2>
                <p>
                    Follow these 4 simple steps to spin up your instance in less than 5 minutes:
                </p>

                <div class="feature-grid">
                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Step 1: Register on Developer Portal</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Navigate to <a href="https://developer.servicenow.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">developer.servicenow.com</a> 
                            and click <strong>Sign In / Sign Up</strong>. You can use any personal email address (Gmail, Outlook, etc.).
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Step 2: Request an Instance</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Once logged in, look at the top-right header and click <strong>Request Instance</strong>. 
                            Select the latest available family release (e.g. <em>Washington DC</em> or <em>Xanadu</em>).
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Step 3: Wait for Cloud Spin-Up</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            ServiceNow's cloud infrastructure will automatically provision your dedicated instance within 2 to 3 minutes.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Step 4: Save Your Admin Credentials</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            You will receive your unique instance URL (e.g. <code>https://dev12345.service-now.com</code>), 
                            username (<code>admin</code>), and temporary password. Save these in your password manager!
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 3: The 10-Day Inactivity Rule -->
            <section class="content-section" id="inactivity-rule">
                <h2>The 10-Day Inactivity Rule &amp; Hibernation Management</h2>
                <p>
                    Because ServiceNow hosts millions of free developer instances globally, they enforce resource management policies:
                </p>

                <div class="feature-grid">
                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>PDI Hibernation (Sleep Mode)</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            If you do not use your PDI for a few hours, it goes into sleep mode to conserve server memory. 
                            To wake it up, visit <a href="https://developer.servicenow.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">developer.servicenow.com</a> 
                            and click <strong>Wake Instance</strong> (takes 60 to 90 seconds).
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>The 10-Day Reclamation Timer</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            If you do not log into your PDI for <strong>10 consecutive days</strong>, ServiceNow will reclaim the instance 
                            and wipe it to free up capacity for other learners.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-card info-card--important">
                    <div class="info-card-title">Pro-Tip: How to Protect Your Code</div>
                    <p>
                        Always capture your custom work in <strong>Update Sets</strong> and export the XML file to your computer. 
                        If your instance is ever reclaimed, you can request a new PDI and restore all your tables and scripts in 30 seconds by importing the XML!
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 4: Next Experience UI Tour -->
            <section class="content-section" id="ui-tour">
                <h2>Next Experience UI Tour (Anatomy of the Interface)</h2>
                <p>
                    Modern ServiceNow uses the <strong>Next Experience Unified Navigation</strong> layout:
                </p>

                <div class="feature-grid">
                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>1. Unified Navigation Banner</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            The persistent dark banner at the top of the browser. Houses the instance logo, the global search bar, 
                            the navigation menus, and your user profile avatar.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>2. The "All" Filter Navigator</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Clicking <strong>All</strong> in the top left opens the master navigation drawer, listing every application, 
                            table, and module in the system (e.g. <em>Incidents, Change, Service Catalog, System Definition</em>).
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>3. Favorites &amp; History Drawers</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            • <strong>Favorites (Star icon):</strong> Pin frequently accessed tables for one-click access.<br>
                            • <strong>History (Clock icon):</strong> Instant timeline of the last 30 records and forms you viewed.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>4. System Settings (Gear Icon)</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Customize your interface theme (Dark Mode / Light Mode), adjust accessibility settings, set timezone preferences, 
                            and toggle compact list view densities.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 5: Filter Navigator Shortcuts -->
            <section class="content-section" id="navigator-shortcuts">
                <h2>Filter Navigator Pro Shortcuts</h2>
                <p>
                    Instead of scrolling through hundreds of menus in the <em>All</em> drawer, professional ServiceNow developers 
                    type direct table command shortcuts in the Filter Navigator box:
                </p>

                <table class="data-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Command Syntax</th>
                            <th>Example Command</th>
                            <th>What It Does</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>&lt;table&gt;.list</code></td>
                            <td><code>incident.list</code></td>
                            <td>Opens the List view of that table in the <strong>current browser frame</strong>.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>&lt;table&gt;.LIST</code></td>
                            <td><code>incident.LIST</code></td>
                            <td>Opens the List view of that table in a <strong>brand new browser tab</strong>.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>&lt;table&gt;.do</code></td>
                            <td><code>incident.do</code></td>
                            <td>Opens a <strong>blank new record form</strong> directly without loading the list first.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>&lt;table&gt;.config</code></td>
                            <td><code>incident.config</code></td>
                            <td>Opens the master configuration dashboard listing all Business Rules, Client Scripts, UI Policies, and Dictionary fields for that table.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>sys_user.list</code></td>
                            <td><code>sys_user.list</code></td>
                            <td>Instantly jumps to the master User Directory table.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 6: Impersonation Key -->
            <section class="content-section" id="impersonation">
                <h2>The Admin Impersonation Key (🔑 Superpower)</h2>
                <p>
                    One of the most powerful built-in tools for ServiceNow administrators and developers is <strong>Impersonation</strong>:
                </p>

                <div class="feature-grid">
                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>What is Impersonation?</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            As an administrator, clicking your profile avatar $\rightarrow$ <strong>Impersonate User</strong> lets you temporarily 
                            view the entire system through the eyes of any other user in the company (e.g. an end user, a manager, or a tier-1 agent).
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Why is it Essential?</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            It allows you to instantly verify whether your security rules (ACLs), catalog visibility policies, 
                            and custom form layouts work correctly for standard employees without needing their passwords or logging out.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-card info-card--tip">
                    <div class="info-card-title">How to End Impersonation</div>
                    <p>
                        When you are done testing, simply click the user avatar in the top right and select <strong>Unimpersonate</strong> 
                        to return immediately to your full <code>admin</code> profile.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

